{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > 3.2 PERFORMANCE COMPARISON\n- Neighbor Text: To systematically evaluate model performance across varying document lengths, we conducted comprehensive experiments on both datasets. As illustrated in Figure 2, RAG-Anything and MM-GraphRAG exhibit comparable performance on shorter documents. However, RAG-Anything’s advantages become increasingly pronounced as document length grows. On DocBench, the performance gap expands dramatically to over 13 points for documents exceeding 100 pages (68.2% vs. 54.6% for 101–200 pages; 68.8% vs. 55.0% for 200+ pages). On MMLongBench, RAG-Anything demonstrates consistent improvements across all length categories, achieving accuracy gains of 3.4 points for 11–50 pages, 9.3 points for 51–100 pages, and 7.9 points for 101–200 pages. These findings confirm that our dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval mechanism is particularly effective for long-document reasoning tasks.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/26ef73a064b6acf1bdd6641af7ee50a43ff4e56177cd68f1cb306cbc7863f3d2.jpg\nCaptions: None\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a line chart titled 'MMLongBench Accuracy' in bold black text at the top. The chart compares the performance of two methods across different document length categories. The y-axis is labeled 'Accuracy (%)' in bold, with a scale from 0 to 100 in increments of 20 (0, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100). The x-axis is labeled 'Page Range' in bold, with categories: '1-10', '11-50', '51-100', '101-200', and '200+'. A legend in the upper right corner identifies two lines: a blue line with circular markers labeled 'RAGAnything' and an orange line with circular markers labeled 'MMGraphRAG'. The chart includes a light gray grid for readability. The blue line (RAGAnything) starts at approximately 18% for '1-10', rises to about 44% for '11-50', dips to 40% for '51-100', stays at 40% for '101-200', and increases to 60% for '200+'. The orange line (MMGraphRAG) starts at about 12% for '1-10', rises to 40% for '11-50', drops to 30% for '51-100', rises to 34% for '101-200', and reaches 40% for '200+'. The chart visually demonstrates that RAGAnything consistently outperforms MMGraphRAG, with the performance gap widening as document length increases, particularly for the '200+' page range. This aligns with the surrounding text's claim that RAG-Anything excels in long-context scenarios, achieving accuracy gains of 3.4 points for 11–50 pages, 9.3 points for 51–100 pages, and 7.9 points for 101–200 pages on MMLongBench."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > A.3 CONTEXT-AWARE MULTIMODAL PROMPTING\n- Neighbor Text: Figure 10 presents the standardized prompt specifically designed for systematic factual accuracy assessment of generated responses across multiple domains. The prompt establishes explicit evaluation criteria that prioritize content correctness over stylistic considerations, producing binary accuracy classifications accompanied by concise analytical justifications. All accuracy evaluations throughout our comprehensive experimental framework were conducted using GPT-4o-mini, ensuring consistent and reliable assessment standards across diverse question categories and specialized domains. A.5 CHALLENGES AND FUTURE DIRECTIONS FOR MULTI-MODAL RAG While current multimodal RAG systems demonstrate promising capabilities, their limitations emerge most clearly through systematic analysis of failure cases. Understanding where and why these systems break down is crucial for advancing the field beyond current performance plateaus. Examining failure patterns helps identify fundamental architectural bottlenecks and design principles for more robust multimodal systems. Our investigation reveals two critical failure patterns exposing deeper systemic issues in multimodal RAG architectures. These patterns are not merely edge cases but reflect fundamental challenges in cross-modal information integration and structural reasoning:\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/5644b5d4113a7939aabe7ac6a37e73fc020e03eef37d97f85aa8bff89f585a4d.jpg\nCaptions: Figure 10: Accuracy evaluation prompt for consistent factual assessment across question types. A.4 ACCURACY EVALUATION PROMPT DESIGN\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image displays a screenshot of a text editor window containing a prompt template titled 'Accuracy Evaluation Prompt'. The window has a light gray background with a title bar at the top showing the filename 'accuracy_evaluation_prompt.png' and standard macOS window control buttons (red, yellow, green) on the left. The main content is a numbered list of instructions and criteria for evaluating the accuracy of answers generated by a RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) system. The text is formatted with markdown-style bold headers and placeholders in curly braces. The prompt is structured into several sections: an introductory role definition, a task description, input fields for the question, expected answer, and generated answer, evaluation criteria with a binary scoring system (0 or 1), specific instructions for handling partial matches, numerical answers, list answers, and unanswerable questions, and finally an output format specification requiring a JSON object with 'accuracy' and 'reasoning' fields. The text is black on a white background, with line numbers on the left side. The overall layout is clean and typical of a code or text editor, with no additional graphical elements. This figure corresponds to Figure 10 in the surrounding document, which describes the standardized prompt used for systematic factual accuracy assessment across multiple domains, emphasizing content correctness over style and producing binary classifications with justifications. The prompt is designed to be used with GPT-4o-mini for consistent evaluation across diverse question categories."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > 3.4 CASE STUDIES\n- Neighbor Text: Multimodal documents contain rich structural information within each modality. Understanding these intra-modal structures is crucial for accurate reasoning. We analyze two representative cases from DocBench to demonstrate how RAG-Anything leverages these structures. These cases highlight a key limitation of existing methods. Baselines either rely on superficial textual cues or flatten complex visual elements into plain text. In contrast, RAG-Anything builds modality-aware graphs that preserve essential relationships (e.g., table header↔cell↔unit edges; panel↔caption↔axis edges). This enables precise reasoning over complex document layouts. • Case 1: Multi-panel Figure Interpretation. This case examines a common scenario in academic literature. Researchers often need to compare results across different experimental conditions. These results are typically presented in multi-panel visualizations. Figure 3 shows a challenging t-SNE visualization with multiple subpanels. The query requires distinguishing between two related but distinct panels. RAG-Anything constructs a visual-layout graph where panels, axis titles, legends, and captions become nodes. Key edges encode semantic relationships. Panels contain specific plots. Captions provide contextual information. Subfigures relate hierarchically. This structure guides the retriever to focus on the style-space panel for comparing cluster separation patterns. The system avoids confusion from the adjacent content space panel. This panel shows less clear distinctions. • Case 2: Financial Table Navigation. This case addresses a common challenge in financial document analysis. Analysts must extract specific metrics from tables with similar terminology and multiple time periods. Figure 4 shows this scenario. The query involves resolving ambiguous financial terms and selecting the correct column for a specified year. RAG-Anything transforms the financial report table into a structured graph. Each row header, column header (year), data cell, and unit becomes a node. The edges capture key relationships: row-of, column-of, header-applies-to, and unit-of. This structure enables precise navigation. The retriever focuses on the row “Wages and salaries” and the column for “2020”. It directs attention to the target cell (26,778 million). The system successfully disambiguates nearby entries like “Share-based payments.” Competing methods treat tables as linear text. They often confuse numerical spans and years. This leads to significantly inaccurate answers. RAG-Anything explicitly models relationships within the table. It achieves precise selection and numeric grounding. This ensures accurate responses. • Key Insights. Both cases demonstrate how RAG-Anything’s structure-aware design delivers targeted advantages. Our approach transforms documents into explicit graph representations. These graphs capture intra-modal relationships that traditional methods miss. In figures, connections between panels, captions, and axes enable panel-level comparisons. This goes beyond keyword matching. In tables, row–column–unit graphs ensure accurate identification through modeling.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/8ce722cfb7edac35a7d9035d234c09ad80cc9ca4af2c205bb7ca00c024457148.jpg\nCaptions: Figure 4: Financial table navigation case. The query involves locating the specific intersection of “Wages and salaries” row and “2020” column amid similar terminological entries.\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a horizontal composite figure illustrating a financial table navigation case study from the RAG-Anything paper. It is divided into three main sections: a document/table evidence area on the left, a set of baseline model answers in the middle, and the RAG-Anything answer on the right.\n\n**Left Section: Multimodal Document and Evidence Table**\n- At the top, a question is posed in a rounded rectangle: 'Question: What was Novo Nordisk's total amount spent on wages and salaries in 2020?' Below it, in parentheses, is the hint: '(Identifying the true evidence is the key)'.\n- Below the question, a label reads '2.4 Employee costs'.\n- A small thumbnail image labeled 'Multimodal Document' shows a multi-page financial report with a highlighted section. A black arrow points from this thumbnail to an enlarged table labeled 'Evidence table in the document'.\n- The evidence table has a header row with columns: 'DKK million', '2020', '2019', '2018'. The first column contains row labels: 'Wages and salaries', 'Share-based payment costs (note 5.1)', 'Pensions – defined contribution plans', 'Pensions – defined benefit plans (note 3.5)', 'Other social security contributions', 'Other employee costs'.\n- The row 'Wages and salaries' is highlighted with a red rectangle around the value '26,778' in the 2020 column. The full row values are: 26,778 (2020), 25,335 (2019), 25,259 (2018).\n- Other rows contain values: Share-based payment costs: 823, 363, 414; Pensions – defined contribution plans: 1,961, 1,910, 1,791; Pensions – defined benefit plans: 138, 151, 73; Other social security contributions: 1,862, 1,963, 1,901; Other employee costs: 2,044, 2,203, 2,087.\n\n**Middle Section: Baseline Model Answers**\n- Three baseline models are shown with their answers, each preceded by a thinking face emoji (🤔).\n- 'GPT-4o-mini 🤔: Novo Nordisk's total amount spent on wages and salaries in 2020 was DKK 32,928 million.'\n- 'MMGraphRAG 🤔: Novo Nordisk spent a total of 11,503 million DKK on wages and salaries in 2020.'\n- 'LightRAG 🤔: Novo Nordisk spent DKK 11,503 million on wages and salaries in 2020.'\n\n**Right Section: RAG-Anything Answer**\n- 'RAG-Anything(Correct 🤔): Novo Nordisk's total amount spent on wages and salaries in 2020 was DKK 26,778 million.' The word 'Correct' is in green, and the answer matches the highlighted cell in the evidence table.\n\n**Overall Context**\nThe figure demonstrates how RAG-Anything correctly navigates a financial table to find the intersection of the 'Wages and salaries' row and '2020' column, yielding the correct value of 26,778 million DKK, while baseline models produce incorrect answers (32,928, 11,503, 11,503). This aligns with the case study description in the surrounding text, which explains that RAG-Anything transforms the table into a structured graph with row headers, column headers, data cells, and units as nodes, enabling precise retrieval and disambiguation of similar terms."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/d462838e30a55f01869a9c8ede71175f3966021ff85384a14e9af8858bdb2202.jpg\nCaption: Table 2: Accuracy (%) on DocBench Dataset. Performance results with best scores highlighted in dark blue and second-best in light blue. Domain categories include Academia (Aca.), Finance (Fin.), Government (Gov.), Legal Documents (Law), and News Articles (News). Document types are categorized as Text-only (Txt.), Multimodal (Mm.), and Unanswerable queries (Una.).\nStructure: <table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Method</td><td colspan=\"5\">Domains</td><td colspan=\"3\">Types</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Overall</td></tr><tr><td>Aca.</td><td>Fin.</td><td>Gov.</td><td>Law.</td><td>News</td><td>Txt.</td><td>Mm.</td><td>Una.</td></tr><tr><td>GPT-4o-mini</td><td>40.3</td><td>46.9</td><td>60.3</td><td>59.2</td><td>61.0</td><td>61.0</td><td>43.8</td><td>49.6</td><td>51.2</td></tr><tr><td>LightRAG</td><td>53.8</td><td>56.2</td><td>59.5</td><td>61.8</td><td>65.7</td><td>85.0</td><td>59.7</td><td>46.8</td><td>58.4</td></tr><tr><td>MMGraphRAG</td><td>64.3</td><td>52.8</td><td>64.9</td><td>40.0</td><td>61.5</td><td>67.6</td><td>66.0</td><td>60.5</td><td>61.0</td></tr><tr><td>RAGAnything</td><td>61.4</td><td>67.0</td><td>61.5</td><td>60.2</td><td>66.3</td><td>85.0</td><td>76.3</td><td>46.0</td><td>63.4</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: The table presents accuracy results on the DocBench dataset, comparing four methods: GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAGAnything. The table is structured with a two-level header: the first row groups columns into 'Domains' (Aca., Fin., Gov., Law., News) and 'Types' (Txt., Mm., Una.), plus an 'Overall' column. The second row specifies the individual domain and type categories. The methods are listed in rows, with accuracy percentages in each cell. Best scores are highlighted in dark blue and second-best in light blue, as per the caption. Key data points: RAGAnything achieves the highest overall accuracy at 63.4%, followed by MMGraphRAG at 61.0%, LightRAG at 58.4%, and GPT-4o-mini at 51.2%. In domain-specific results, RAGAnything leads in Finance (67.0%) and News (66.3%), while MMGraphRAG leads in Academia (64.3%) and Government (64.9%), and LightRAG leads in Law (61.8%). For document types, RAGAnything and LightRAG tie for best on Text-only (85.0%), RAGAnything significantly outperforms on Multimodal (76.3% vs. next best 66.0% by MMGraphRAG), and MMGraphRAG leads on Unanswerable (60.5%). The table supports the surrounding content's claim that RAG-Anything demonstrates superior overall performance and cross-domain generalization, particularly excelling in multimodal document understanding. The results validate the architectural design choices discussed in the ablation studies, showing that graph construction and cross-modal integration are essential for handling multimodal documents."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > A.5 CHALLENGES AND FUTURE DIRECTIONS FOR MULTI-MODAL RAG\n- Neighbor Text: • Document Structure Processing Challenges: Systems struggle with complex layouts and nonlinear information flows. This exposes limitations in spatial reasoning and contextual understanding. These failure modes illuminate key insights about current multimodal AI. They provide concrete directions for architectural innovations that could substantially improve system robustness. Case 1: Cross-Modal Misalignment. Figure 11 presents a particularly revealing failure scenario where all evaluated methods consistently produce incorrect answers despite having access to the necessary information. This universal failure across different architectures suggests fundamental limitations in how current systems handle noisy, heterogeneous multimodal data—a critical challenge as real-world applications inevitably involve imperfect, inconsistent information sources. The failure exposes two interconnected systemic issues that compound each other: Issue 1: Retrieval Bias Toward Text. Current RAG systems demonstrate pronounced bias toward textual passages. This occurs particularly when visual content lacks exact keyword matches. The bias persists even when queries contain explicit instructions to prioritize visual sources. This reveals a fundamental weakness in cross-modal attention mechanisms. The retrieved textual information, while topically related, often operates at a different granularity level than visual content. Images may contain precise, structured data such as specific numerical values, detailed diagrams, or exact spatial relationships. Corresponding text typically provides general, conceptual descriptions. This semantic misalignment introduces noise that actively misleads the reasoning process. The system attempts to reconcile incompatible levels of detail and specificity.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/6156188856f546acdadbd384576f30cb30d1a4eefc826a76b874f8689e4408b5.jpg\nCaptions: Figure 11: Cross-modal noise case. All methods fail to retrieve the correct answer from the specified image, instead retrieving noisy textual evidence that misaligns with the structured visual content. Figure 12: Ambiguous table structure case. All methods fail to correctly parse the confusing table layout with merged cells and unclear column boundaries, leading to incorrect data extraction.\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a composite figure illustrating a failure case in multimodal RAG systems, labeled as Figure 11: Cross-modal noise case. It is divided into three main sections: a left panel showing the source document and question, a middle panel displaying the evidence table, and a right panel listing the incorrect responses from four different methods.\n\n**Left Panel:**\n- At the top, a question is posed: 'Question: Which model's style space shows a clearer separation between different styles according to Figure 2?'\n- Below the question, a small image of a 'Multimodal Document' is shown, depicting a page with text and a figure (Figure 2). A blue arrow points from this document to the evidence table.\n- The evidence table is titled 'Evidence table in the document' and has a note '(An irregular table)' above it. The table is structured with merged cells and unclear boundaries. It contains the following data:\n  - Header row: 'Evaluation on 4 Domains', 'Joint', 'Slot', 'Joint', 'Slot'\n  - Sub-header: '', 'Except Hotel', 'Except Train', '', ''\n  - Row 1: 'Base Model (BM) training on 4 domains', '58.98', '96.75', '55.26', '96.76'\n  - Row 2: 'Fine-tuning BM on 1% new domain', 'Native', '36.08', '93.48', '23.25', '90.32'\n  - Row 3: '', 'EWC', '40.82', '94.16', '28.02', '91.49'\n  - Row 4: '', 'GEM', '53.54', '96.27', '50.69', '96.42'\n  - Row 5: 'Evaluation on New Domain', 'Hotel', 'Train'\n  - Row 6: 'Training 1% New Domain', '19.53', '77.33', '44.24', '85.66'\n  - Row 7: 'Fine-tuning BM on 1% new domain', 'Native', '19.13', '75.22', '59.83', '90.63'\n  - Row 8: '', 'EWC', '19.55', '76.25', '60.10', '90.33'\n  - Row 9: '', 'GEM', '19.73', '77.92', '53.31', '89.85'\n\n**Middle Panel:**\n- Contains two responses from different models, each with a robot emoji.\n- 'GPT-4o-mini 🤖: The Joint goal accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved by 6.26% when using the GEM fine-tuning strategy compared to training from scratch, achieving 65.35% versus 59.09%.'\n- 'MMGraphRAG 🤖: The Joint goal accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved from 44.24% when training from scratch to 59.83% when using the GEM fine-tuning strategy, resulting in an improvement of 15.59%.'\n\n**Right Panel:**\n- Contains two more responses, each with a robot emoji.\n- 'LightRAG 🤖: The Joint goal accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved from 44.24% using training from scratch to 50.51% with GEM fine-tuning, indicating an improvement of 6.27%.'\n- 'RAG-Anything 🤖: The Joint Goal Accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved from 44.24% with training from scratch to 58.10% using the GEM fine-tuning strategy, resulting in an improvement of 13.86%.'\n\nAll responses provide incorrect answers, demonstrating the failure of all methods to correctly interpret the irregular table and retrieve the correct information. The figure highlights the challenges of cross-modal noise and structural ambiguity in multimodal RAG systems."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > 3.2 PERFORMANCE COMPARISON\n- Neighbor Text: Superior Performance and Cross-Domain Generalization. RAG-Anything demonstrates superior overall performance over baselines through its unified multimodal framework. Unlike LightRAG, which is restricted to text-only content processing, RAG-Anything treats text, images, tables, and equations as first-class entities. MMGraphRAG only adds basic image processing while treating tables and equations as plain text, missing crucial structural information. RAG-Anything introduces a comprehensive dual-graph construction strategy that preserves structural relationships across all modalities. This unified approach enables superior performance across both evaluation benchmarks. Enhanced Long-Context Performance. RAG-Anything demonstrates superior performance on long-context documents. The framework excels where relevant evidence is dispersed across multiple modalities and sections. It achieves the best results in information-dense domains such as Research Reports and Financial Reports on MMLongBench. These improvements stem from the structured context injection mechanism. This mechanism integrates dual-graph construction for cross-page entity alignment. It combines semantic retrieval with structural navigation. The framework also employs modality-aware processing for efficient context window utilization. Unlike baselines that cannot uniformly process diverse modalities, RAG-Anything effectively captures scattered multimodal evidence. Its cross-modal hybrid retrieval architecture combines structural knowledge navigation with semantic similarity matching. This enables the framework to leverage both explicit relationships and implicit semantic connections across modalities. To systematically evaluate model performance across varying document lengths, we conducted comprehensive experiments on both datasets. As illustrated in Figure 2, RAG-Anything and MM-GraphRAG exhibit comparable performance on shorter documents. However, RAG-Anything’s advantages become increasingly pronounced as document length grows. On DocBench, the performance gap expands dramatically to over 13 points for documents exceeding 100 pages (68.2% vs.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/f31dea98118298da9874431172c90b6519da2a5a5cb74847779484f96a73aa97.jpg\nCaptions: None\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a line graph titled 'DocBench Accuracy' that compares the performance of two models, 'RAGAnything' and 'MMGraphRAG', across different document lengths. The graph's x-axis is labeled 'Page Range' and is divided into five categories: '1-10', '11-50', '51-100', '101-200', and '200+'. The y-axis is labeled 'Accuracy (%)' and ranges from 0 to 100 in increments of 20. A legend in the top right corner identifies the two lines: a blue line with circular markers represents 'RAGAnything', and an orange line with circular markers represents 'MMGraphRAG'. The graph shows that for shorter documents (1-10 pages), MMGraphRAG has a slightly higher accuracy (around 65%) compared to RAGAnything (around 62%). For the 11-50 page range, both models perform similarly at approximately 62%. For the 51-100 page range, RAGAnything's accuracy remains stable at around 62%, while MMGraphRAG's accuracy drops to about 60%. A significant divergence occurs for longer documents. In the 101-200 page range, RAGAnything's accuracy increases to approximately 68%, while MMGraphRAG's accuracy drops further to around 55%. For documents over 200 pages, RAGAnything maintains its high accuracy at roughly 69%, whereas MMGraphRAG's accuracy remains low at about 55%. This graph visually supports the text's claim that RAG-Anything demonstrates superior performance on long-context documents, with the performance gap expanding dramatically for documents exceeding 100 pages."}
